AB  - Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) is a set of software engineering practices which uses a ubiquitous language, one that business and technical people can understand, to describe and model a system by a series of textual scenarios. However, we believe that the value of those textual scenarios is connected with how well they convey and document the details discussed by the team about the behaviors needed to fulfill customer needs. Thus, there is the need to define what would be a "good" BDD scenario and how to evaluate scenarios. On this research design paper, we describe a study designed to understand how known quality attributes from other types of requirements specifications (like use cases or user stories) can be used with BDD scenarios. We also report our preliminary findings after performing it with novice evaluators. Those findings indicate that some of those attributes can be used to support BDD scenarios assessments.
