AB  - Ensuring software quality in agile and continuous integration environments has become increasingly challenging due to the complexity and pace of modern development. This paper explores the use of Large Language Models (LLMs) in automating software testing and validation processes. We present an AI-based framework leveraging Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and prompt engineering to automatically generate Gherkin scenarios from Jira Xray requirements within a Behavior-Driven Development (BDD) context. Following the CRISP-DM methodology, the system integrates semantic retrieval, generative modeling, and human-in-the-loop validation to ensure syntactic compliance, semantic fidelity, and industrial applicability. Experimental results show that the proposed approach achieves a high syntactic accuracy, BLEU score, and an 87% reduction in test generation time compared to manual methods. These findings demonstrate the feasibility and effectiveness of LLM-driven automation in bridging natural language requirements with executable test artifacts, marking a significant advancement in AI-augmented software quality assurance.
ER  - 

